# The /arc-* skill stubs are GENERATED from src/arc-slash.js (stubText) and a test
# asserts byte-equality against the generator. This repo has no committed line-ending
# policy and Windows checkouts default to core.autocrlf=true, which would rewrite the
# stubs to CRLF on a fresh clone and fail that drift guard on every machine. Pin them.
skills/*/SKILL.md text eol=lf

# A WORKFLOW SCRIPT IS DATA, NOT A FILE THE HARNESS COMPILES. It is handed to the Workflow tool,
# whose permission layer REFUSES any script containing control characters — "script contains control
# characters that would be hidden in the approval dialog". A CR (0x0D) is a control character, so a
# Windows checkout of a workflow script (580 CRLFs in round.js) is rejected outright: `inquiry` could
# not launch AT ALL on arc's only supported platform. Reproduced verbatim before pinning this.
#
# It hid well. Both the repo copy and the deployed copy are byte-identical and "scan clean", because
# a scan that treats CR as ordinary line-ending noise — which every normal scan does — never counts
# the 580 control characters it is looking for. Same class as the stubs above, one severity higher:
# the stubs failed a test, this failed the feature.
bundles/*/workflows/*.js text eol=lf

# THE SAME CLASS, A THIRD TIME. A python `io.open(path,"w")` on Windows translates \n to \r\n, and one
# edit rewrote test/run.js end-to-end: an 8,599-line diff hiding a 324-line change. The commit was
# never at risk (core.autocrlf=true, index uniformly LF) — REVIEW LEGIBILITY was, and it was caught by
# eyeballing a diffstat rather than by anything structural. The working tree was measurably mixed
# (28 CRLF files, 11 LF) while the index was clean. Pin it, the way the stubs and the workflows above
# were each pinned after an incident of exactly this shape.
*.js text eol=lf
